#!/bin/sh
#
# Every submodule pointer this repo records must exist on that submodule's
# remote before the parent pointer is pushed.
#
# Skipping one push aborts EVERY CI job that checks out submodules recursively
# -- seventeen of them here -- at checkout, before a test compiles, with a
# message about `upload-pack: not our ref` that names none of it. Locally
# everything looks fine, because the commit is right there.
#
# The check cannot live in CI: by the time any job runs, checkout has already
# succeeded, which means the pointers were pushed. A CI copy could only ever
# report success.
#
# Installed by the root `prepare` script, which npm runs on install. It used to
# say "Enable with: git config core.hooksPath .githooks" and rely on someone
# doing that by hand; nobody had, so this hook -- written for this exact failure,
# naming this exact cost -- had never run once. Run 33376013146 then lost all 74
# jobs to precisely it.
# Two questions, and only the first had a guard.
#
#   1. Is the pointer fetchable?          check-submodule-pointers-pushed.mjs
#   2. Does the pointer name YOUR work?   check-submodule-work-is-committed.mjs
#
# The second is what a `git add -A && git commit` in the parent silently gets
# wrong: it records a pointer, not the edits sitting in the submodule's working
# tree. Preflight then passes -- it reads that working tree -- and CI checks out
# the commit the pointer names, without them. A Rust fix and the gate enforcing
# it landed in one wave that way; CI failed on the gate, naming the very fields
# the fix had changed.
node scripts/check-submodule-work-is-committed.mjs || exit 1
exec node scripts/check-submodule-pointers-pushed.mjs
